Yes, good practice. Here's the full setup:

**1. Create and activate the venv:**

```bash
cd "C:\Users\admin\Downloads\database\renewal_advisor"
python -m venv .venv
.venv\Scripts\activate
```

**2. Create the requirements.txt:**

```bash
pip install -e packages/sra_pricing -e packages/sra_rules pytest hypothesis
pip freeze > requirements.txt
```

**3. From now on, whenever you open a new terminal, just activate it first:**

```bash
.venv\Scripts\activate
```

**Then run the tests:**

```bash
python -m pytest packages/sra_rules/tests/ packages/sra_pricing/tests/ -v
```

That's it. The venv keeps all packages isolated to this project so it doesn't affect anything else on your machine. The `requirements.txt` means anyone else (or your manager) can recreate the same environment with just `pip install -r requirements.txt`.
